        "explanation": "The DELIM_AND_VALUE object inside the exported function defines how property values are parsed after the key delimiter. Its nested starts.contains rule ({ begin: '\\\\\\\\n' }) is intended to recognize an escaped newline (i.e. a backslash at end of line) and continue the string. However, this pattern does not distinguish between a true continuation (single \u2018\\\\\u2019 before newline) and a literal escaped backslash (\u2018\\\\\\\\\u2019), so when a value ends with \u2018\\\\\u2019 it still matches and treats it as a continuation. Adjusting or refining this rule in DELIM_AND_VALUE is necessary to correctly handle trailing escaped backslashes without extending the value to the next line.",
        "bug_scenario": "When a property value ends with an escaped backslash (\"\\\\\"), the existing highlight.js grammar for .properties files mistakes it for a line-continuation marker. Instead of stopping the string at the end of that line, it carries the value onto the next line, coloring it as part of the multi-line value."
